(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-pharm-exam-1-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"Pharmacology I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form D","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which set matches the nicotinic effects?","choices":["Sedation, hyperthermia, flushing, dry mouth and urinary retention","Vasodilation, atony of the bladder, cycloplegia and xerostomia","Mydriasis, tachycardia, weakness, hypertension and fasciculations","Miosis, bradycardia, bronchospasm, lacrimation and salivation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and note this set moves the pupil and heart in the opposite direction to the muscarinic set. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A patient stops clonidine abruptly and the blood pressure spikes. What is this?","choices":["Malignant hypertension of pregnancy","Rebound hypertension","Pheochromocytoma","Serotonin syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Never stop clonidine abruptly. — 03. ANS Pharmacology, Slide 97"},{"q":"Which organism does griseofulvin fail to cover, and what is the nature of its action?","choices":["It is not effective against Candida, and it is fungistatic rather than fungicidal","It is not effective against dermatophytes, and it is fungicidal","It is not effective against Aspergillus, and it is fungicidal against Candida","It covers all fungi but only when given topically"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is effective against numerous dermatophytes, which is a different target population entirely. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"Which atypical organisms do macrolides cover?","choices":["Rickettsiae, Borrelia burgdorferi and Brucella","Legionella, Mycoplasma, Chlamydophila, Chlamydia","Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter, Burkholderia, Stenotrophomonas","Bacteroides fragilis and Clostridium perfringens"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and this atypical coverage is what makes them a community-acquired pneumonia agent. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which toxicities define the aminoglycoside class?","choices":["Serotonin syndrome, hypertensive crisis, and thrombocytopenia","Renal toxicity and ototoxicity, requiring renal dose adjustment","Hepatotoxicity and thrombocytopenia","Tendon rupture and peripheral neuropathy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and vancomycin shares both, which is why the combination is watched carefully. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A patient with a true penicillin allergy needs gram-negative cover. Which agent is safe?","choices":["Meropenem","Piperacillin","Aztreonam","Ceftriaxone"],"answer":2,"exp":"No documented cross-reactivity with beta-lactams. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which agents are the gold standard for atopic dermatitis?","choices":["Oral corticosteroids","Sedating antihistamines","Topical corticosteroids","Topical immunomodulators"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with the agent chosen by severity and by site of disease. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which sequence describes neurotransmission at a cholinergic neuron?","choices":["Synthesis, storage, release, and inactivation by catechol-O-methyltransferase","Storage, release, binding, and permanent removal of choline from the neuron","Synthesis, storage, release, receptor binding, degradation, recycling of choline","Synthesis, release, reuptake, repackaging"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Six sequential steps, and several drug classes act at one of them. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which organisms do tetracyclines cover excellently?","choices":["Enterococcus and Listeria monocytogenes only","Anaerobes exclusively, with no aerobic activity","Atypicals and animal-borne organisms","Pseudomonas, C. difficile, E. faecium, Acinetobacter"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Poor coverage is specified for Pseudomonas and Clostridium difficile. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which azoles are noted as teratogenic?","choices":["Ketoconazole and posaconazole only","None of the azoles carries a teratogenicity caution","Only the topical imidazole derivatives","Itraconazole, and fluconazole and voriconazole in animals"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Reproductive safety is a recurring caution across the azole group. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Through which receptor does epinephrine produce bronchodilation?","choices":["Alpha-two","Beta-two","Beta-one","Alpha-one"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, the same receptor albuterol targets selectively. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What enzyme do the imidazoles and triazoles inhibit?","choices":["Thymidylate synthase, blocking DNA synthesis","Fungal cytochrome P450 14-alpha-demethylase, which converts lanosterol to ergosterol","Squalene epoxidase, which acts early in the fungal ergosterol synthesis pathway","1,3-beta-D-glucan synthase in the cell wall"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Because the target is a cytochrome P450 enzyme, this class interferes with human cytochrome P450 enzymes too, which is the source of its drug interactions. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"What are atropine's effects on the eye?","choices":["Persistent mydriasis and cycloplegia","Miosis with spasm of accommodation","Reduced aqueous humour production with no change in pupil size","No ocular effect at any dose"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is the opposite of the miosis and accommodation spasm the agonists cause. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A patient applies tretinoin each morning and burns. What is the advice?","choices":["Apply it at night","Apply it twice daily","Add benzoyl peroxide","Stop all acne therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Tretinoin is photolabile. — 02. Dermatology Medications, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is pyridostigmine's clinical role?","choices":["Diagnosis of myasthenia gravis","Reactivating acetylcholinesterase after organophosphate exposure","Reducing intraocular pressure in glaucoma","Chronic management of myasthenia gravis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, as distinct from edrophonium's diagnostic role. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which central nervous system effects are listed for ganciclovir?","choices":["Peripheral neuropathy and tendon injury","Blurred vision and mental confusion from enzyme induction","Confusion, ataxia, seizures and coma","Seizures, delirium and tremor only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, occurring in a minority of patients alongside the haematologic effects. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"Which adverse effects limit flucytosine?","choices":["Tendon rupture and peripheral neuropathy","Bone marrow suppression, hepatotoxicity, gastrointestinal disturbance and rash","Nephrotoxicity and electrolyte wasting requiring hydration with normal saline","Visual disturbance in about 30 percent of patients"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is available orally only. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"Besides blocking norepinephrine reuptake, what else does cocaine do?","choices":["It inhibits monoamine oxidase","It depletes vesicular stores of norepinephrine","It stimulates dopaminergic receptors in the kidney","It blocks neuronal sodium channels"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it also blocks sodium-potassium activated ATPase. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Compared with acyclovir, what makes ganciclovir the harder drug to tolerate?","choices":["Severe infusion reactions requiring premedication","A disulfiram-like reaction on any alcohol exposure","Neutropenia and thrombocytopenia; a third stop treatment","Crystals in the renal tubule; hydrate throughout"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acyclovir's bone marrow suppression is listed, but ganciclovir's is far more limiting. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"Which infusion-related reaction is characteristic of vancomycin?","choices":["Red man syndrome, along with fever, chills and phlebitis","Disulfiram-like reaction when combined with ethanol","Serotonin syndrome when combined with a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor","Tendonitis and Achilles tendon rupture"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it sits alongside ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity among the monitoring concerns. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What is mupirocin used for beyond ordinary skin infection?","choices":["Eliminating nasal carriage of Staphylococcus aureus","Eliminating vulvovaginal candidiasis in women","Treating recurrent orolabial herpes simplex","Treating onychomycosis of the toenails"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and its coverage is most gram positive aerobes, especially meticillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Why are fever and chills expected during an amphotericin B infusion, and how are they managed?","choices":["Fungal lysis releasing endotoxin, fixed by slowing the rate","A disulfiram-like reaction to the vehicle","Interleukin-1 and tumour necrosis factor; pre-medicate","An IgE allergic reaction; stop the drug permanently"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The reaction is a cytokine response rather than an allergy, which is why premedication works. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"What are the two proposed actions of azelaic acid?","choices":["Antimicrobial, and blocks testosterone conversion","Inhibition of fungal cell wall formation and keratinolysis","Converts to benzoic acid, with a comedolytic peeling effect","Inhibition of T cell activation and mast cell stabilisation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the lecture is explicit that the mechanism is not fully understood. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What does the afferent division do, and what is an example?","choices":["It controls gastrointestinal motility independently of the brain","It carries signals to the central nervous system","It carries signals away from the central nervous system to skeletal muscle","It connects preganglionic to postganglionic neurons within a ganglion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The reflex arc needs the incoming limb before any autonomic outflow can be adjusted. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Why give a small dose of a nondepolarizing blocker before succinylcholine?","choices":["To allow a lower dose of anaesthetic agent","To prevent the fasciculations that cause muscle soreness","To prolong the duration of the block","To prevent malignant hyperthermia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is a comfort measure that addresses a predictable adverse effect. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Three prodrug and parent pairs appear in the antiviral section. Which pairing is correct?","choices":["Valacyclovir-ganciclovir, famciclovir-acyclovir, valganciclovir-penciclovir","Oseltamivir-acyclovir, valacyclovir-penciclovir, famciclovir-ganciclovir","There are no prodrugs among the antivirals in this lecture","Valacyclovir-acyclovir, famciclovir-penciclovir, valganciclovir-ganciclovir"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In each case the prodrug exists to solve poor oral absorption of the parent. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"What is the position on isotretinoin in pregnancy?","choices":["Contraindicated in pregnancy and breastfeeding; men too","Safe after the first trimester","It is contraindicated only in the first trimester","It requires no pregnancy precautions because it is applied topically"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the iPledge programme exists around exactly this risk. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is the collateral consequence of fluoroquinolone overuse?","choices":["Permanent loss of Gram-positive coverage across the entire class","Cross-resistance to all beta-lactams","High rates of resistance, and Clostridium difficile as collateral damage","Accelerated hepatic metabolism of co-administered drugs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The slide states overuse equals resistance in capital letters, which is a deliberate emphasis. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What kind of infection are the allylamines used for?","choices":["Superficial dermatophyte infections","Systemic mycoses of the internal organs","Cryptococcal meningitis","Helminth infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Naftifine is topical and terbinafine is available orally and topically. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"How are acne lesions divided?","choices":["Noninflammatory comedones; inflammatory papules and nodules","Noninflammatory as papules; inflammatory as comedones","Superficial as pustular; deep as comedonal","Acute as nodules and pustules; chronic as comedones"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That split is what decides whether topical or systemic therapy is chosen. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which laboratory change does isotretinoin cause?","choices":["Reduced serum calcium","Raised serum immunoglobulin E","Reduced white cell count","Increased serum lipids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it sits alongside retinoid dermatitis, photophobia, arthralgia, headaches, alopecia and brittle nails. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is intravenous labetalol used for?","choices":["Septic shock","Hypertensive emergencies","Chronic open-angle glaucoma","Acute asthma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, using its combined alpha and beta blockade. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"How does reserpine act?","choices":["It blocks transmitter uptake into vesicles","It blocks release of stored norepinephrine from vesicles","It blocks reuptake of norepinephrine at the terminal","It inhibits monoamine oxidase within the neuron"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, depleting norepinephrine, dopamine and serotonin. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"Which cephalosporin provides methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus coverage?","choices":["Cefepime, a fourth generation agent","Ceftolozane/tazobactam, a fifth generation agent","Ceftriaxone, a third generation agent","Ceftaroline, a fifth generation agent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is the cephalosporin that breaks the class's usual absence of methicillin-resistant coverage, and it must be renally adjusted. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A patient has used Afrin for a week and is more congested than before. What is this?","choices":["Allergic rhinitis","Sinusitis","Nasal polyps","Rhinitis medicamentosa"],"answer":3,"exp":"Rebound congestion past three days. — 03. ANS Pharmacology, Slide 95"},{"q":"Which non-neurologic adverse effects are listed for acyclovir?","choices":["Neutropenia and thrombocytopenia as the dominant effects","Photosensitivity and tooth discoloration","Disulfiram-like reaction with ethanol","Nausea, vomiting, rash and bone marrow suppression"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with hydration and renal function to be maintained because of tubular crystallisation. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"Which additional effect does carbachol produce through its nicotinic action?","choices":["Depletion of stored norepinephrine","Release of epinephrine from the adrenal medulla","Direct blockade of the neuromuscular junction","Inhibition of acetylcholinesterase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is why its cardiovascular and gastrointestinal effects can be profound and biphasic. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What happens to newly formed influenza virions when neuraminidase is inhibited?","choices":["They can no longer attach to the receptor on the next host cell","They cannot uncoat once inside the next cell","Their nucleic acid cannot be replicated","They cannot be cleaved free of the host cell as budding progeny"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — they remain tethered at the surface, so the infection cannot spread to new cells. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"A patient asks how well ciclopirox nail lacquer works for onychomycosis. What do you say?","choices":["Almost always curative","It works within two weeks","Under 12% effective","About half of patients"],"answer":2,"exp":"The figure the deck gives. — 02. Dermatology Medications, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which antiviral approach does oseltamivir represent?","choices":["Inhibiting viral release","Blocking viral attachment to cells","Inhibiting nucleic acid synthesis","Stimulating the host immune system"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — neuraminidase frees budding progeny, so blocking it traps new virus at the cell surface. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"What does nicotine do at the nicotinic receptor as concentration rises?","choices":["It blocks the receptor at all concentrations","It has no effect on the nicotinic receptor, acting only at muscarinic sites","It stimulates the receptor at low concentrations and blocks it at high concentrations","It stimulates the receptor at all concentrations, increasing with dose"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is why nicotine appears in both the agonist and the ganglionic blocker discussions. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is metronidazole's mechanism of action?","choices":["It inhibits dihydrofolate reductase","It interacts with bacterial DNA to cause loss of helical structure and strand breakage","It inhibits DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV","Binds 50S and blocks transpeptidation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and its activity spans Gram-positive and Gram-negative anaerobes plus parasites. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Twenty minutes into a vancomycin infusion a patient flushes red over the face and neck and itches. What is this?","choices":["Stevens-Johnson syndrome","Serum sickness","Red man syndrome","Anaphylaxis"],"answer":2,"exp":"An infusion-rate reaction, not an allergy. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals, Slide 51"},{"q":"Through which receptor does amphetamine raise blood pressure?","choices":["Dopaminergic","Alpha-one","Beta-two","Alpha-two"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with beta-one accounting for the cardiac stimulation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"A farm worker is bradycardic, salivating and wheezing after pesticide exposure. Which drug reverses the muscarinic effects?","choices":["Pralidoxime","Neostigmine","Physostigmine","Atropine"],"answer":3,"exp":"The antidote for cholinesterase inhibitor insecticides. — 03. ANS Pharmacology, Slide 51"},{"q":"What distinguishes tolnaftate's spectrum from the other topical antifungals?","choices":["Works on pedis, cruris and corporis, but not unguium","It is effective against tinea unguium of the nails but against none of the other tineas","It covers Candida but no dermatophytes","It covers systemic mycoses when given orally"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It works by distorting hyphae and stunting mycelial growth, and is topical only. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"What is the depot effect, and what does it allow?","choices":["The vehicle evaporates and leaves crystals that dissolve slowly in sweat","Repeated applications saturate hair follicles and shut down absorption","Skin acts as a reservoir, allowing once daily dosing","Stored in fat and released on exercise"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The reservoir is what lets a short acting drug be given less often than its half-life suggests. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which two alpha-one blockers are used for benign prostatic hyperplasia?","choices":["Tamsulosin and alfuzosin","Prazosin and terazosin","Phentolamine and phenoxybenzamine","Labetalol and carvedilol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, while prazosin, terazosin and doxazosin are listed for hypertension. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"Which macrolide-containing regimen is named for Helicobacter pylori?","choices":["Azithromycin with doxycycline","Erythromycin with metronidazole and omeprazole","Prevpac — clarithromycin, amoxicillin and lansoprazole","Clarithromycin with ethambutol and rifabutin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A macrolide, a penicillin and a proton pump inhibitor together. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A beta blocker begins with a letter from N to Z. What does that suggest?","choices":["Cardioselective","It has alpha blockade","It has intrinsic sympathomimetic activity","Non-selective"],"answer":3,"exp":"A to M are generally cardioselective. — 03. ANS Pharmacology, Slide 120"},{"q":"How long does griseofulvin treatment take for scalp, fingernail and toenail infection?","choices":["Scalp a week, fingernails a month, toenails three","Three months for all three sites","A single dose for the scalp, with topical therapy thereafter for nails","Scalp a month, fingernails 6 to 9, toenails up to 12"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The gradient follows how slowly each keratin structure grows out, and it is the counselling point that keeps patients adherent. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"Which are the three polyene agents?","choices":["Caspofungin, micafungin and anidulafungin","Ketoconazole, miconazole and clotrimazole","Griseofulvin, terbinafine and naftifine","Amphotericin B, nystatin and natamycin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Amphotericin B is the systemic agent; nystatin is used topically and orally because it is poorly absorbed. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"Which of the two prevents cardiovascular mortality in heart failure?","choices":["Pindolol","Carvedilol","Labetalol","Propranolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it also decreases lipid peroxidation and vascular wall thickening. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"A patient presents on day 4 of influenza. What limits oseltamivir now?","choices":["It is contraindicated in adults","It must start within 48 hours","It needs a negative rapid test","It cannot be given orally"],"answer":1,"exp":"The window has passed. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals, Slide 126"},{"q":"Which two agents are allylamines, and how do they work?","choices":["Clotrimazole and miconazole, which inhibit fungal cytochrome P450","Nystatin and tolnaftate, binding membrane sterols","Ciclopirox and sertaconazole, which block precursor uptake","Naftifine and terbinafine, inhibiting ergosterol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with local irritation as the adverse reaction. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which penicillin dosage form is given intramuscularly as a single long-acting dose?","choices":["Penicillin G (benzylpenicillin)","Ampicillin sodium","Penicillin G benzathine (Bicillin)","Penicillin V potassium (Pen VK)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The benzathine salt is the depot form, which is why one injection can cover an indication such as syphilis. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"From which plants is atropine derived?","choices":["Deadly nightshade, jimson weed and mandrake","Foxglove and lily of the valley","Opium poppy and coca","Ephedra and willow bark"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and scopolamine comes from the same family. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the anti-inflammatory action of tetracyclines in acne?","choices":["They stabilise mast cell membranes and block histamine release","They inhibit the conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone","They stimulate macrophages to release tumour necrosis factor alpha","They chelate calcium ions, which prevents neutrophil and monocyte chemotaxis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That is an anti-inflammatory action separate from killing the organism. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which receptors does oxymetazoline act on?","choices":["Dopaminergic","Alpha-one and alpha-two","Beta-one and beta-two","Alpha-one only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, producing vasoconstriction. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"What is the rank order of potency at beta receptors?","choices":["Isoproterenol, then epinephrine, then norepinephrine","Epinephrine, then norepinephrine, then isoproterenol","Norepinephrine, then epinephrine, then isoproterenol","All three are equipotent at beta receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The alpha order runs the other way, with epinephrine and norepinephrine far above isoproterenol. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 77"}]};
